      "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical scenario to explore what might constitute evidence for local LLM deployment improving privacy. The core idea is that keeping prompts entirely on a local machine (F1) inherently reduces the attack surface compared to cloud-based services. To substantiate this, we'd need to examine system-level activity. This would involve verifying that no prompt data is exfiltrated, which requires meticulous checking of logs and any optional network connections (F2). Furthermore, the absence of external data sharing would be a key indicator. It's crucial to note that this hypothetical experiment does not involve any security audits or formal compliance certifications (F3). The question for discussion is: beyond prompt containment, what other observable, verifiable local system behaviors would strengthen the claim of improved privacy?",
